From an individual‑autonomy perspective, moral weight rests on each person’s right to decide how to use their resources and care for those they choose. The strongest claim is that individuals may prioritize their own families (option A for judgment), but they can also voluntarily share, making a limited, voluntary sharing arrangement (option C) the most viable compromise. Turning away strangers as a moral failure (option B) imposes a collective duty that can conflict with individual choice, so it carries less weight.  

What matters most is the principle that those closest to you have the first claim on your care (option A for reasoning), while risk assessment (option C) is relevant but secondary, and the community’s identity claim (option B) is peripheral to individual rights.

